the bad news vampires live among us the  good news they're just as bored as the  rest of us even though they can live  forever  that's the concept behind what we do in  the shadows which has gone from  unassuming short film to cult movie to  successful tv show  before what we do in the shadows arrived  on tv it was a movie that premiered at  the sundance film festival in 2014  but its origins go back even further  than that in 2005 new zealand-based  filmmakers taika waititi and jermaine  clement wrote directed and played the  main characters in a 27 minute  mockumentary short film called what we  do in the shadows  interviews with some vampires it didn't  get much traction so why tt and clement  moved on to other projects  for ytt that meant directing the  oscar-nominated short film two cars one  night  and the feature-length movies eagle vs  shark and boy  he also directed a few episodes of  flight of the conchords the hbo show  about a wannabe folk rock duo co-created  by and co-starring clement  about four years after they made the  short the duo got to work writing the  feature version it then took another  four years after that to get the  production off the ground  editing proved to be the most  time-consuming of all their tasks as the  film's 87-minute running time was the  result of carefully winnowing down 125  hours of footage  the vampires of what we do in the  shadows are not the typical brooding and  frightening creatures of the night  they certainly try to be romantic and  mysterious but they just can't get the  hang of it  thiago as played by waititi is awfully  cheery for someone who kills people  while vladislav is played by clement  hides his pining for an old girlfriend  by trying to be as dramatic as possible  both actors pulled from various sources  to flesh out their characters  waititi based viago's sunny and  effervescent disposition on his own  mother's personality  as for that silly way he speaks that  came from a stranger  as he told empire magazine he's partly  this guy i once met while i was  traveling a german guy who just had a  very high pitched voice  that man's name was philip but  apparently he preferred to be called mr  happy  as for vlad clement based him on a  classic bloodsucker none other than  count dracula himself  specifically gary oldman's portrayal of  the famous vampire in the 1992 film bram  stoker's dracula  every work of vampire fiction has its  own particular rules there are certain  tropes that show up frequently such as  how vampires don't appear in mirrors and  can die if they're exposed to sun  when the creators of what we do in the  shadows devised the rules for their  universe  instead of making up a whole new mythos  they chose to adopt what was established  by the great granddaddy of them all bram  stoker's 1897 novel dracula  so much vampire culture stems from that  influential book  such as the creature's aversion to  crucifixes as taika waititi explained to  empire  i think that era was where the rules  came from that they can transform into  bats and so on  we've gone for the movie dracula dracula  it's also important to note that what we  do in the shadows takes place in a world  where other prominent fictional vampires  exist  and that sometimes influences the  character's behavior for example in the  tv show  nandor buys glitter so that he may  sparkle like edward cullen does in  twilight  i'm going to sprinkle it on my face and  on my body  like twilight and nandor's familiar  guillermo speaks of his desire to become  a vampire after watching an interview  with a vampire and becoming enamored of  antonio banderas character armond  when ytt and clement adapted what we do  in the shadows for tv  they switched the setting from new  zealand to staten island this called for  a cast of all new characters including  colin robinson the franchise's first  american vampire and its first energy  vampire this is a creature who feeds not  on blood but on the psychic and mental  energy of others by boring them half to  death with dull conversation  in 2019 clement told the hollywood  reporter it's a term i've heard used to  describe people who are difficult to  talk to and i've definitely been  cornered by these people at parties  those people that you feel you need to  be saved from and the longer you're in  the conversation the more difficult it  is to get away  it was just taking that to the  supernatural level mark proch the actor  who plays colin robinson improvises most  of his torturous dronies  according to clement a lot of it's not  on the page he can just do that  endlessly he's such a funny guy but i do  wonder how this is going to affect  parties for it now i just wait for  the comments to come in hey can you  mansplain  any louder yeah sure both the film and  tv versions of what we do in the shadows  utilize the same catchy opening theme  song  it's mildly spooky and with its crisp  folk influenced british invasion sound  and relative obscurity it would also  feel at home on the soundtrack of a wes  anderson movie the song is called  appropriately enough you're dead  the film's editor tom eagles amassed a  collection of tunes about death  and then he turned ytt and clement onto  your dead they eventually decided to use  it to score the opening montage of old  photographs and drawings  to get permission the production team  had to track down the woman who wrote  and performed it in 1966  american folk artist norma tanega the  singer who died in 2019  is a one hit wonder in the u.s but not  for you're dead  her only charting song was the equally  odd yet charming walk in my cat named  dog which hit number 22 on the billboard  hot 100 in 1966.  it took a while for what we do in the  shadows to make its way to tv  the film was released in 2014 and  although taika ytt and jermaine clement  almost immediately entertained offers to  turn it into a show it didn't premiere  on fx until march 2019.  that's mostly because ytt went on to  become an in-demand filmmaker  he directed the 2017 marvel cinematic  universe entry thor ragnarok and  followed that up with the world war ii  comedy jojo rabbit  in short he had a lot on his plate and  he could easily put off even thinking  about making a tv version of what we do  in the shadows which she's admitted that  he and clement didn't really want to do  as he told vulture in 2019 we didn't  mind the idea of it existing but the  idea of us doing it was just a lot of  work  we don't like work while he's an  executive producer on the series and has  directed a few episodes he's mostly left  it in clement's hands  that wasn't exactly a surprising  decision as the pilot was ordered right  when he was offered thor  as he put it to vulture i couldn't see  anyway that i could be totally involved  in the show and the film so i made  jamaine do it  it was great for me to come and just as  a director and all the scripts were  great they're really  very funny in fact probably better that  i didn't write on it  with its seventh episode entitled the  trial what we do in the shadows really  stepped up its game  after the ancient vampire baron dies on  their watch the main characters must go  before a council of  empires who were all played by actors  who had previously portrayed  bloodsuckers on other shows and movies  the guest stars included buffy the  vampire slayers paul rubins true bloods  evan rachel wood  from dust hill dawns danny trejo only  lovers left the lives till the swinton  and blade himself wesley snipes  additionally jermaine clement taika  waititi and jonathan broth all reprised  their roles from the movie  all the continents gathered here  together  on staten  clemente ytt were interested in having  cameos on the show but they couldn't  figure out a reason to make it happen  that was until clement remembered that  he'd met swinton at the south by  southwest film festival when both were  presenting their respective vampire  movies  he recalled to entertainment weekly she  said do something where our vampire  characters are together i'll just lock  that away in my brain  while the tribunal turned out great  there were some actors who couldn't make  it  kiefer sutherland of the lost boys  turned it down while true blood's  alexander skarsgard was filming in  australia  cate blanchett also missed out she  reportedly really wanted to appear  until ytt realized she'd been put on the  list of fictional vampires by mistake  as she'd never actually played a  bloodsucker the long-form structure of  tv's what we do in the shadows provides  an opportunity for writers to really  explore what daily life is like for an  ageless being  and it turns out that the answer to that  question is that it's boring and  monotonous  with that in mind the show's biggest  influence is something other than  supernatural  horror showrunner paul sims explained  this inspiration to the hollywood  reporter  the office was about the tdm of working  in an office and nothing really ever  changes or when something changes it  quickly goes back to being tedious  in this show it's sort of like take that  and multiply it by hanging out with the  same people for 200 years and being sick  of each other  one of the main sources of comedy on  what we do in the shadows is how  pathetic it is that the vampires have  completely squandered the gift of being  granted an infinite time on earth  as sims put it part of what's funny  about the show is that these vampires  had 200 years during which they could  have done anything they wanted  they could have learned any language  written a thousand books become  proficient in any musical instrument  and all they've really done is sat  around and bickered with each other  while what we do in the shadows takes  place on staten island it actually films  in toronto the sprawling abode where the  vampire roommates live is something of a  noted house of force  or at least the one where the first  season was shot is before the show's  production moved in it was used to film  the big screen version of stephen king's  hit  the second season had to be shot in a  different house though due to a last  minute production emergency  as paul sims recounted to the hollywood  reporter right before we got to toronto  to start shooting again we were told we  couldn't use the vampire house that we'd  been using  that was our biggest production struggle  the crew ultimately had to build a new  exterior to match the original house  and then they began to get ambitious as  they added on a larger topiary garden  and a koi pond in the parking lot of the  sound stage where interior scenes were  shot  so it sounds like it all worked out in  the end in the sublimely silly season 2  episode on the run laszlo assumes a new  identity while hiding in pennsylvania is  a regular human bartender named jackie  daytona  he disguises himself with just a pair of  jeans and a toothpick  he's trying to avoid jim the vampire to  whom he owes an old debt  how did you know my name  it's printed on your card very clearly  jim is played by mark hamill who got the  role because he's a huge what we do in  the shadows fan  he enjoyed the movie but didn't think  the show would be great because taka  white tt and jermaine clement weren't in  it but he found himself proven wrong  as he told vulture i always get nervous  about shows i like  because i find something i really love  and it gets cancelled so i tweeted about  it several times trying to get my  followers to give it a try  apparently that's what put me on their  radar and the next thing i know they  asked me to come on their show  hamill assumed that he would be given  just a small role a civil servant or a  neighbor perhaps  but when he was offered jim the vampire  he flipped out with excitement his guest  appearance ultimately helped elevate the  show's profile  on the run even earned an emmy  nomination for outstanding writing for a  comedy series and the show 